<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Downstream Applications</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show two potential downstream applications of our work in policy optimization shown below and multimodal action planning shown in Appendix Sec. <a href="#S7.SS8.SSS10" title="7.8.10 Downstream Application2: Multi-Sensory Action Planning ‣ 7.8 Additional Experiments and Discussion ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ MultiModal Action Conditioned Video Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8.10</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low-level Policy Optimization</span>

One downstream application of our proposed action-conditioned video generative simulator is to optimize a policy of low-level actuation.
Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, We set up task as goal-conditioned policy optimization, where we optimize a policy to generate a trajectory of low-level actuation <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{[1,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{[1,T]}</annotation></semantics></math> that brings the environment from start state <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to target <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. States are described by images <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\doteq x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≐</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\doteq x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We show one use case of our model in goal-conditioned policy optimization. We compare training of the same policy network <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\cdot)_{\pi_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\cdot)_{\pi_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> under two conditions. First, we define the baseline method using the commonly employed goal-conditioned policy training approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. This baseline is the policy network taking the starting state and target state, depicted by two video frames <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and directly regress policy <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> minimizing the L2 distance between the predicted action <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}[1,T]=\pi_{\theta}(x_{0},x_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}[1,T]=\pi_{\theta}(x_{0},x_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> and ground truth expert action trajectory<math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{[1,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{[1,T]}</annotation></semantics></math>. This L2 loss term is defined as <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{a}=\sum_{t}\|\hat{a}_{t}-a_{t}\|_{2}=\|p(x_{0},x_{T})_{\pi_{\theta}}-a_{[1,T]}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{a}=\sum_{t}\|\hat{a}_{t}-a_{t}\|_{2}=\|p(x_{0},x_{T})_{\pi_{\theta}}-a_{[1,T]}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The second condition is to train the same policy <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> in conjunction with our pretrained simulator. We feed the action trajectory predicted by policy network <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}_{[1,T]}=\pi_{\theta}(x_{0},x_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}_{[1,T]}=\pi_{\theta}(x_{0},x_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> into our pretrained simulator model <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to predict the video frames from this action trajectory <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{T}=g(p(x_{0},x_{T})_{\pi_{\theta}})_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{T}=g(p(x_{0},x_{T})_{\pi_{\theta}})_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. This additional loss term is defined as <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{sim}=\|\hat{x}_{T}-x_{T}\|_{2}=\|g(p(x_{0},x_{T})_{\pi_{\theta}})_{T}-x_{T}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{sim}=\|\hat{x}_{T}-x_{T}\|_{2}=\|g(p(x_{0},x_{T})_{\pi_{\theta}})_{T}-x_{T}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The total loss term for the second condition is <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{simpolicy}=\mathcal{L}_{a}+\mathcal{L}_{sim}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{simpolicy}=\mathcal{L}_{a}+\mathcal{L}_{sim}</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate the effectiveness of by using L2 distance between the predicted action <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}_{[1,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}_{[1,T]}</annotation></semantics></math> and ground truth action <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{a}_{[1,T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{a}_{[1,T]}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{a}_{[1,T]}-{a}_{[1,T]}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{a}_{[1,T]}-{a}_{[1,T]}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> MSE is a supporting metric that compares target state and the simulated end state using our simulator. Unfortunately, no other multisensory action simulator exist to use for further validation.
We see from Fig. <a href="#S3.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 3.3 Ablation Experiments ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ MultiModal Action Conditioned Video Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> that adding our additional supervision signal helps to improve policy optimization. Directly regressing multi-sensory actions with a policy network is difficult because the action space in our task setting is quite large, 2292 dimensional. More results are shown in Fig. <a href="#S7.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 7.8.9 Additional discussion and results on downstream application ‣ 7.8 Additional Experiments and Discussion ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ MultiModal Action Conditioned Video Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> in Appendix Sec. <a href="#S7.SS8.SSS9" title="7.8.9 Additional discussion and results on downstream application ‣ 7.8 Additional Experiments and Discussion ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ MultiModal Action Conditioned Video Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8.9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we introduce the concept of multisensory interaction for fine-grained generative simulation.
We focus on learning effective multisensory feature representations to effectively control a downstream video generative simulator.
Our proposed multimodal feature extraction paradigm along with regularization scheme to extract action feature vectors capable of accurately controlling video prediction and robust to missing modalities at test time.
We conduct extensive comparisons, ablations, and downstream applications to showcase the merits of our method.
We hope our work brings insights and inspirations to the research community.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
